Abstract

This document defines how MOS represents, observes, models, and reasons about the external world.

The World Model extends cognition beyond internal memory by introducing explicit representations of environments, observations, objects, states, processes, and interactions.

MOS SHALL distinguish between:

internal cognition,
external reality,
simulated reality,
predicted reality.

This distinction allows reasoning systems to evaluate uncertainty, maintain situational awareness, and interact safely with complex environments.

1. Purpose

Previous RFCs define cognition.

This RFC defines what cognition is about.

MOS introduces the concept of a World Model:

A continuously evolving semantic representation of the environments in which cognitive actors operate.

2. World Axiom

MOS defines:

Every observation SHALL refer to a world context.

No observation exists without context.

Example:

Bad:

Temperature: 42°C

Good:

Temperature:
42°C

Environment:
Factory A

Sensor:
Temperature Sensor 12

Timestamp:
2026-08-03 10:00 UTC

Confidence:
0.96
